  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Coastal low pressure tonight and a cold front \nMonday delivery some rain, with cooler conditions to follow.\n\nA complex and messy area of low pressure near the Southeast will\nlift up off the East Coast tonight ahead of a cold front \napproaching from the Midwest. The area of low pressure had 3 \ndistinct centers as of mid afternoon: one east of NC, one \noffshore of SC, and one south of New Orleans. Given the strung \nout nature of the pattern, most heavier activity will remain \neast toward the immediate coast and north toward southeastern \nNew England. Enough moisture and lift on the western edge of \nthis broad system will continue to result in some sprinkles and \nlight rain mainly east of the Blue Ridge/Catoctin Mountains \nthrough this evening. After midnight, the cold front approaching\nfrom the Midwest delivers a potential for showers and sprinkles\nto the Appalachians and perhaps east toward I-81. Overall \nrainfall amounts will be light and have continued to trend \ndownwards. In fact, a subset of the latest model guidance has \nmore rain with scattered shower activity along the cold front \nMonday than with the coastal low fringes tonight.\n\nThe cold front will cross the region Monday as low pressure\noffshore moves further away. Lingering moisture and lift ahead\nof an embedded shortwave may help to generate a few showers.\nOtherwise, more clouds than sun is expected with northwest\nbreezes. Cold advection lags, so the downsloping wind may push\ntemperatures to near 70 in some lower elevation locations Monday\nafternoon. Much cooler (and drier) air barrels in Monday night\non northwest breezes. The colder than normal temperatures linger\ninto the middle of the week ahead of the next storm system.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Rainy and breezy conditions possible over the \nThanksgiving holiday as a coastal low tracks up the coast.\n\nBehind multiple cold fronts, temperatures on Tuesday cool and return \nto late-November normals persisting throughout the week. High \npressure builds over the Ohio River Valley giving our area a brief \nbreak in rainfall until Wednesday evening.\n\nModels continue to favor an elongated trough digging into the \nMississippi River Valley Wednesday with an associated upper- \nlevel low becoming cut off on Thursday. This trough sets up \nanother coastal low pressure system to trail up the Mid-Atlantic\nover Thanksgiving. This far out, both deterministic and \nensemble solutions differ whether this system tracks up the east\nside of the Appalachian Mountains or remains off the Mid- \nAtlantic coastline. Since last night, model trends favor a more \nwestern track with greater rainfall accumulation and stronger \nwinds. At this time, either solution would lead to gusty winds \nand soaking rains. Depending on how fast this system can move \ninto new England, precipitation diminishes overnight into Friday\nmorning. Breezy/gusty conditions could persist through the end \nof next week.\n\nAcross the Alleghenies, mixed wintry precipitation remains a \npossibility Thanksgiving Day, particularly at the highest \nelevations. If this were to occur, it would be brief with\nmarginal temperatures likely keeping any accumulating snowfall \nminimal. As the coastal low pressure pulls away, mixed wintry \nprecipitation/snow showers could return to the Alleghenies and \nlinger through Friday, though low-level wind direction currently\nappears unfavorable for significant snow.\n\nHigh pressure develops in the wake of this system bringing \nseasonable, dry conditions east of the Alleghenies Friday and \ninto the start of the weekend.",
